1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,730
Ask three friends to list the
good students in your class.

2
00:00:03,730 --> 00:00:05,762
You will get three different lists.

3
00:00:06,162 --> 00:00:10,317
Now ask them to list the students
who ride a bicycle to college.

4
00:00:10,317 --> 00:00:12,478
All three lists match, exactly.

5
00:00:12,928 --> 00:00:15,842
Aarav noticed this in the
college corridor today.

6
00:00:16,192 --> 00:00:17,807
Same class, same friends.

7
00:00:17,807 --> 00:00:20,704
Why does one list work,
and the other one fail?

8
00:00:21,204 --> 00:00:25,162
The answer is the very first
idea of Class 11 maths.

9
00:00:25,162 --> 00:00:26,853
It is called a set.

10
00:00:27,503 --> 00:00:28,783
So what is a set?

11
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:32,357
A set is a well defined
collection of objects.

12
00:00:32,757 --> 00:00:35,605
Well defined means it
passes one simple test.

13
00:00:35,605 --> 00:00:40,039
For any object, everyone can say
clearly: is it in, or is it out?

14
00:00:40,439 --> 00:00:41,829
Watch the test run.

15
00:00:41,829 --> 00:00:44,561
Our rule is: even numbers less than ten.

16
00:00:44,961 --> 00:00:45,861
One?

17
00:00:45,449 --> 00:00:47,336
It is odd, so it is out.

18
00:00:47,336 --> 00:00:48,236
Two?

19
00:00:47,824 --> 00:00:49,595
Even, and less than ten.

20
00:00:49,595 --> 00:00:50,495
It is in.

21
00:00:50,838 --> 00:00:52,962
Every number gets a clear answer.

22
00:00:52,962 --> 00:00:56,054
So the collection is
two, four, six and eight.

23
00:00:56,054 --> 00:00:57,173
That is a set.

24
00:00:57,823 --> 00:00:58,916
Side by side.

25
00:00:58,916 --> 00:01:00,888
Students who ride a bicycle.

26
00:01:00,888 --> 00:01:03,185
Kiran rides one, so she is in.

27
00:01:03,185 --> 00:01:05,168
Joel walks, so he is out.

28
00:01:05,168 --> 00:01:06,352
Everyone agrees.

29
00:01:06,752 --> 00:01:07,738
Good students.

30
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:08,780
Is Meera good?

31
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:11,196
One friend says yes, another says no.

32
00:01:11,196 --> 00:01:14,201
There is no clear answer,
so it is not a set.

33
00:01:14,801 --> 00:01:15,767
Now you try.

34
00:01:15,767 --> 00:01:19,685
Is the collection of months that
begin with the letter J a set?

35
00:01:19,685 --> 00:01:20,885
Pause and decide.

36
00:01:24,085 --> 00:01:24,985
Yes!

37
00:01:24,629 --> 00:01:26,484
January, June and July.

38
00:01:26,484 --> 00:01:30,375
Anyone who checks a calendar
gets the same three months.

39
00:01:31,025 --> 00:01:34,888
The objects inside a set are
called its elements, or members.

40
00:01:35,238 --> 00:01:38,079
We name a set with a
capital letter, like A.

41
00:01:38,079 --> 00:01:40,578
Its elements are small letters or numbers.

42
00:01:40,978 --> 00:01:44,939
If two is in A, we write two,
then this curly symbol, then A.

43
00:01:44,939 --> 00:01:46,776
It reads: two belongs to A.

44
00:01:47,526 --> 00:01:50,788
If three is not in A, we
strike the symbol through.

45
00:01:50,788 --> 00:01:52,583
Three does not belong to A.

46
00:01:53,333 --> 00:01:54,341
Remember this.

47
00:01:54,341 --> 00:01:58,674
A set is a collection with a clear
in or out answer for every object.

48
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:02,852
Some sets are used so often
that they get their own letters.

49
00:02:03,202 --> 00:02:04,863
N is the natural numbers.

50
00:02:04,863 --> 00:02:07,179
One, two, three, and on forever.

51
00:02:07,579 --> 00:02:09,073
Z is the integers.

52
00:02:09,073 --> 00:02:11,835
It adds zero and the negative numbers.

53
00:02:12,235 --> 00:02:14,338
Q is the rational numbers.

54
00:02:14,338 --> 00:02:19,692
Every number p over q, where p and
q are integers and q is not zero.

55
00:02:20,092 --> 00:02:21,724
R is the real numbers.

56
00:02:21,724 --> 00:02:24,267
Every single point on the number line.

57
00:02:24,917 --> 00:02:25,817
Quick one.

58
00:02:25,806 --> 00:02:27,646
Is zero a natural number?

59
00:02:27,646 --> 00:02:28,903
Pause and think.

60
00:02:32,103 --> 00:02:33,003
No!

61
00:02:32,595 --> 00:02:34,733
Natural numbers start at one.

62
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:37,587
Zero first appears in Z, the integers.

63
00:02:38,237 --> 00:02:40,281
Now, how do we write a set down?

64
00:02:40,281 --> 00:02:41,491
There are two ways.

65
00:02:41,841 --> 00:02:43,715
The first is roster form.

66
00:02:43,715 --> 00:02:48,191
List every element inside curly
brackets, with commas between them.

67
00:02:48,591 --> 00:02:52,520
Our set becomes two, four, six,
eight, inside curly brackets.

68
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,060
The second is set builder form.

69
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:59,029
Instead of listing the elements,
we write the rule they all follow.

70
00:02:59,429 --> 00:03:00,783
Read it like this.

71
00:03:00,783 --> 00:03:05,429
The set of all x, such that x is an
even natural number, and x is less

72
00:03:05,429 --> 00:03:06,329
than ten.

73
00:03:06,978 --> 00:03:09,162
The curly bracket means the set of.

74
00:03:09,162 --> 00:03:10,787
x stands for any element.

75
00:03:10,787 --> 00:03:12,457
The colon means such that.

76
00:03:12,457 --> 00:03:14,406
After the colon comes the rule.

77
00:03:15,156 --> 00:03:17,184
Now watch the rule do the work.

78
00:03:17,184 --> 00:03:19,440
Change the limit from ten to twenty.

79
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,894
The rule stays short, but
the list keeps growing.

80
00:03:22,894 --> 00:03:25,821
That is why set builder
form wins for big sets.

81
00:03:26,471 --> 00:03:27,624
Side by side.

82
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:30,197
Roster form shows you every element.

83
00:03:30,197 --> 00:03:32,237
It is best for small sets.

84
00:03:32,637 --> 00:03:35,045
Set builder form shows you the rule.

85
00:03:35,045 --> 00:03:39,470
It is best for large or endless sets,
like all the real numbers between

86
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:40,522
zero and one.

87
00:03:41,122 --> 00:03:42,858
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:43,208 --> 00:03:48,044
A is the set of all x, such that x
is a prime number less than twenty.

89
00:03:48,044 --> 00:03:49,732
Write A in roster form.

90
00:03:49,732 --> 00:03:50,864
Is nine in A?

91
00:03:51,364 --> 00:03:52,264
Step one.

92
00:03:52,222 --> 00:03:56,095
A prime number has exactly
two factors, one and itself.

93
00:03:56,845 --> 00:03:57,745
Step two.

94
00:03:57,662 --> 00:04:00,275
Test every number from two to nineteen.

95
00:04:01,025 --> 00:04:01,927
Step three.

96
00:04:01,927 --> 00:04:06,693
The primes are two, three, five,
seven, eleven, thirteen, seventeen and

97
00:04:06,693 --> 00:04:07,593
nineteen.

98
00:04:08,188 --> 00:04:09,088
Step four.

99
00:04:09,062 --> 00:04:10,921
Nine is three times three.

100
00:04:10,921 --> 00:04:14,498
It has three factors, so
nine does not belong to A.

101
00:04:15,248 --> 00:04:18,548
So A has eight elements, and
two is the only even one.

102
00:04:19,198 --> 00:04:20,664
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:21,014 --> 00:04:25,005
Write the set of integers x such
that x squared is less than ten.

104
00:04:25,005 --> 00:04:27,406
Many students write one, two, three.

105
00:04:27,806 --> 00:04:30,534
But integers include
zero and the negatives.

106
00:04:30,534 --> 00:04:34,203
Minus three squared is nine,
which is also less than ten.

107
00:04:34,203 --> 00:04:37,967
The full set has seven elements,
from minus three to three.

108
00:04:38,617 --> 00:04:40,069
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:40,069 --> 00:04:44,710
In JEE Main, as questions that give
a set in set builder form and ask how

110
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:46,211
many elements it has.

111
00:04:46,811 --> 00:04:47,960
Exam level now.

112
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:50,268
How many elements are in this set?

113
00:04:50,268 --> 00:04:51,465
Pause and solve.

114
00:04:56,865 --> 00:04:58,230
The answer is C.

115
00:04:58,230 --> 00:04:59,255
Twenty five.

116
00:04:59,755 --> 00:05:02,926
If n is even, n squared minus one is odd.

117
00:05:02,926 --> 00:05:05,916
An odd number is never divisible by eight.

118
00:05:06,416 --> 00:05:11,648
If n is odd, n minus one and n plus
one are two even numbers in a row.

119
00:05:11,648 --> 00:05:14,226
One of them is a multiple of four.

120
00:05:14,726 --> 00:05:18,978
So their product is a multiple of
two times four, which is eight.

121
00:05:18,978 --> 00:05:23,832
Every odd n from one to forty nine works,
even one, because zero is divisible

122
00:05:23,832 --> 00:05:24,732
by eight.

123
00:05:24,628 --> 00:05:26,521
That is twenty five numbers.

124
00:05:27,121 --> 00:05:28,580
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:28,580 --> 00:05:30,079
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:33,279 --> 00:05:36,116
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:36,516 --> 00:05:37,416
One.

128
00:05:36,997 --> 00:05:39,089
Why is good students not a set?

129
00:05:39,989 --> 00:05:40,889
Two.

130
00:05:40,509 --> 00:05:43,446
Write the vowels of
English in roster form.

131
00:05:44,346 --> 00:05:45,246
Three.

132
00:05:44,919 --> 00:05:47,773
What does the colon mean
in set builder form?

133
00:05:48,873 --> 00:05:51,453
Next time: the word SCHOOL has two O's.

134
00:05:51,453 --> 00:05:53,749
So why does its set have only one?

135
00:05:53,749 --> 00:05:56,574
We find out in roster
and set builder forms.
